@@s001 Least squares and projections
@@s002 dato:4 measurements,ora:a trial line
@@s003 dato:observed y,due:predicted Xw,no:residuals,ok:the loss
@@s004 dato:data,ok:least squares line,no:residuals
@@s005 The four points.
@@s006 The <b>least squares</b> line: the one that minimises the sum of the squares of the residuals. Result: <b>y = 1.1 + 1.1x</b>.
@@s007 The residuals (in red) are the vertical distances between points and line: −0.1, 0.8, −1.3, 0.6. Sum of squares = <b>2.7</b>, the smallest possible.
@@s008 Two facts that are not a coincidence: the residuals <b>sum to 0</b> (−0.1 + 0.8 − 1.3 + 0.6 = 0), and their inner product with the x is 0 (0·(−0.1) + 1·0.8 + 2·(−1.3) + 3·0.6 = 0). The vector of residuals is <b>orthogonal</b> to the columns of X. It is the key of the next section.
@@s009 due:column x (and its span),dato:observed y,ok:ŷ = projection,no:residual
@@s010 dato:XᵀX,due:Xᵀy,ok:w*
@@s011 dato:X (4 × 2),due:Xᵀ,ok:X⁺ and w
@@s012 ok:same result,due:more information
@@s013 Least squares and projections
@@s014 With real data there are more points than parameters and the system has no exact solution. The "first milestone" of the slides: "solve/learn linear least squares optimization for model parameters with more data than model parameters". Here: the problem, the loss in matrix form, the solution, its geometry (a <b>projection</b>), the normal equations, the pseudoinverse and NumPy.
@@s015 The problem: more points than parameters
@@s016 A model that is linear in the parameters is written <span class="m">Xw = y</span>, with one row of X for each measured point. If the points are more than the parameters (X is tall and narrow), the system is <b>overdetermined</b>: with real data, which contain noise, no w makes all the equations true together.
@@s017 It makes no sense to ask for the "right" w. The question becomes: which w <b>gets it wrong least</b>? To answer one needs a number measuring the overall mistake of a w, and the course uses the sum of the squares of the errors, the same loss as week 1.
@@s018 No line passes through all four
@@s019 The loss in matrix form
@@s020 With the design matrix all the predictions are obtained together: <span class="m">Xw</span> is the vector of predictions, one per point. The vector of <b>residuals</b> is <span class="m">r = y − Xw</span>, and the loss is the sum of the squares of its components, that is its squared norm:
@@s021 The loss to minimise
@@s022 True value minus predicted value.
@@s023 The sum of squares is the squared norm of the vector of residuals (<a href="w01-loss-minimi-quadrati.html">least squares loss</a>, <a href="w02-norme-distanze.html">norm</a>).
@@s024 argmin = "the value of w that makes the expression minimal". The asterisk on w indicates the optimal solution.
@@s025 Compared with the formula with the Σ of week 1 only the division by N is missing, which does not change where the minimum is.
@@s026 y − Xw, then the squared norm
@@s027 The least squares solution
@@s028 Among all the lines there is only one with the smallest sum of squares: the <b>least squares line</b>. For the four points (0, 1), (1, 3), (2, 2), (3, 5) it is y = 1.1 + 1.1x, with sum of squares 2.7.
@@s029 It passes through no point, and that is fine: it lies "in the middle" of the data, balancing the errors. Two properties are seen at once in the residuals: they sum to zero (the line is not all above or all below) and they have zero inner product with the column of x. They are not coincidences: they say that the vector of residuals is <b>orthogonal</b> to the columns of X, and this is where the formula comes from.
@@s030 The best line for 4 points
@@s031 The geometry: projecting y onto the column space
@@s032 To see what happens, the drawing is changed: instead of an axis for x and one for y, one uses <b>an axis for each measurement</b>. With N measurements one works in ℝᴺ; y (the N measurements) is a vector, and so is each column of X.
@@s033 Why the solution is a projection
@@s034 Column view: the possible predictions are all the linear combinations of the columns of X, a subspace (the <b>column space</b>).
@@s035 Otherwise there would be an exact solution.
@@s036 Like the shadow of a vector on a line (<a href="w02-prodotto-interno.html">inner product</a>). "Closer" = minimum distance = least squares.
@@s037 This is why in the section above the residuals had inner product 0 with the column of 1s and with that of the x.
@@s038 The derivation of the pseudoinverse
@@s039 The indications of week 4: the derivation (DW section 6.2) "is not required for the exam", but the geometric interpretation is. At the oral one must be able to say: the residuals are orthogonal to the column space, so Xw* is the projection of y.
@@s040 Two measurements, one parameter: the projection is seen
@@s041 The normal equations
@@s042 The condition "the residual is orthogonal to every column of X" is written in one line: <span class="m">Xᵀ(y − Xw) = 0</span>, because each row of Xᵀ is a column of X and the product does all the inner products together. Expanding:
@@s043 These are the <b>normal equations</b> ("normal" in the sense of perpendicular). The advantage: XᵀX is <b>square</b>, of size parameters × parameters, whatever the number of points. From a tall and impossible system one passes to a square and (usually) solvable system. For the line, XᵀX contains N, Σx and Σx², and Xᵀy contains Σy and Σxy: it is exercise 14 of the test.
@@s044 XᵀX and Xᵀy for the 4 points
@@s045 The pseudoinverse
@@s046 Solving the normal equations: <span class="m">w* = (XᵀX)⁻¹Xᵀy</span>. The matrix in front of y has a name:
@@s047 It is the <b>pseudoinverse</b> (of Moore-Penrose): it does for tall matrices what the inverse does for square ones, and w* = X⁺y. Be careful however: X⁺X = I, but XX⁺ is not the identity, it is the matrix that <b>projects</b> onto the column space. When X is square and invertible, X⁺ coincides with X⁻¹.
@@s048 The shapes are the safest way to remember the formula: X is N × p, Xᵀ is p × N, XᵀX is p × p, X⁺ is p × N, w is p.
@@s049 From a tall matrix to a formula
@@s050 Least squares in NumPy
@@s051 Three ways, same w (verified: [1.1, 1.1])
@@s052 <code>np.c_</code> puts columns side by side: one of 1s (for w₀) and one with the x. It is the design matrix of the <a href="w04-polinomi.html">page on polynomials</a>.
@@s053 The formula of the theory. It works, but XᵀX can be ill-conditioned (numbers that lose precision).
@@s054 The pseudoinverse, computed in a stable way.
@@s055 <code>lstsq</code> solves the least squares problem directly; it also returns the sum of the residuals and the rank. <code>[0]</code> takes only w.
@@s056 solve, pinv, lstsq
